One of the aspects that makes chatbot interactions feel natural is the language that's used by response generators, such as a language model.

Language models are often encountered in everyday applications, such as Windows' Clippy, Siri or Alexa. In recent years, the use and impact of language models have become a significant topic in the data science and AI industry, both because of what they're able to do and because of some of their limitations.

As a broad emotional term, I'd classify my overall experience with language models as being `speechless`, `amused`, `astounded` and `triumphant`.

Being speechless isn't always something that's attributable only to a negative experience. Often, I felt speechless because of the wit and cleverness of the language model, or perhaps it used a double entendre, as Clippy has been observed to do occaisionally. amused is a broad state of being, as can be any comment by a machine that has the direct or indirect result of making you laugh. Astounded is—as a broad emotional term—that state of being when you encounter something that's unexpected, surprising or delightful. triumphant refers to when you feel that the machine has succeeded and when you can say to yourself, "It's done it! It's LinkedIn's SkyNET!"

It's the triumphant and astounded moments that I find most interesting – and that's why I became curious about the mechanics behind crafting responses.

This post and the tests were not conducted scientifically. Nonetheless, they show some of the mechanics behind language models, although they're largely presented for entertainment purposes only and shouldn't be understood as an authoritative overview.

## The central question

I wanted to determine if putting language models in certain dialog modalities could guide the sorts of responses that they give, beyond their inherent capacities.

To answer this question, I used the `text-davinci-001` to engage with other language models and verify that the `text-davinci-001` had indeed changed modalities as a result of my prompt.

The question then becomes whether the change in style has a hand in effecting the sort of response that the other models returns.

## Background

Understanding language models first boils down to the levels of language that make them up.

One distinction commonly made is that between layers of grammar and phonology, semantics and syntax. I was curious about the specific level of language that could be used to affect the outputs of a language model.

Although one could think of phonology—the level of language that's represented by sound or lip movement—as a plausible candidate, I found that it didn't resonate with me as well as another distinction did.

There are two levels of language that I would like to highlight. First, there's the Pragmatic level, which is what I would label the gestalt level. Pragmatics are concerned with the usage of language in a specific context, as well as its social significance. Then, there's the Syntax level, which pertains to grammatical rules that underpin the structure of sentences.

To put this in a plainer English, it's the difference between your tone (inflexion, diction, volume, tempo) and the structure of what you're saying.

