Contributors: Kathryn Schimansky (NYU)
Venue: NAACL 2018

Affiliations: New York University [1]

Notes: preview talk

Last modified: 06/01/2019

Abstract:

Word embeddings like Word2Vec and GloVe have been shown to encode a variety of linguistic phenomena, but there is still a lot of dependence on the availability of word co-occurrence information. Recent state-of-the-art NLP models, like those used in neural machine translation, are typically only trained with end-to-end objectives and do not directly optimize any co-occurrence metric. Such models use attention mechanisms, a general method to summarize source sentence context on a per-target-word basis. The observations from these models have suggested that there are certain attention heads in the model that are able capture a range of complex phenomena, but-to our knowledge-the mechanisms that give rise to these abilities have not yet been extensively studied. In this work, we examine the linguistic information that the attention heads of the Transformer model capture and attempt to derive an intuitive set of rules for why the linguistic phenomena occur. While prior analyses state that Transformer models learn to use attention to perform specialized linguistic tasks, we take this one step further and delve into the peculiarities- Why do I feel like I can use the attention weights to infer parts of speech, but not the semantics of a particular word in context? Are the phenomena I'm identifying on a per-head basis because the head has a singular linguistic function?
[24/200] Running: What is dynamic batching in inference servers?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Batching refers to executing a group of distinct but related requests together as a single process to take advantage of resource utilization.  In a batch request, the API allows multiple inference requests to be submitted in a single call.

Dynamic batching is a batching strategy implemented on the “fly” where similar inference requests submitted separately are intelligently combined together by an inference engine (also called an inference server) into a   send and execute them as a single batch to optimize resource usage. It combines requests based on incoming inference requests as they arrive whilst preserving first arrived, first process behavior.  Compare this with bulk or static batching where  all the inputs to be batched have to be aggregated together before even sending to the inference server. This unspoken requirement of bulk batching makes it supply bound, while dynamic batching is compute bound.

Dynamic batching in inference is a form of scheduling, which leads to inference requests being grouped together to exploit better utilization of model predictions. This allows the computational work to be utilized better by increasing system throughput and reducing system latency than if the requests would have been processed individually, as seen in the following image.

Dynamic batching was initially implemented or innovated by TensorFlow with the model parallel hyperparameter "dynamic_batch_size", where TensorFlow would accumulate incoming shapes and then dispatch a batch of shape dynamically, and then subsequently implemented in TorchServe as part of PyTorch stance towards inference servers and re-implemented by FuseML in its support of AI/ML and HPAI runtimes.

To incorporate this into existing inference infrastructure, dynamic batching needs to be implemented as a part of the inference server, where the inference server accepts direct and data flow in the form of normal function calls and dynamically groups them as they come into the server based on the type of resources required or other factors like similarity of requests and data. This is a huge improvement over the current way where inference servers tend to run one function call at a time, wasting resources.

Key benefits of dynamic batching

- Improved hardware utilization
    - With dynamic batching, servers are better utilized with batch orders. This is especially beneficial in cases of microservices, which increase the number of smaller services, leading to server cycles being wasted and could run into compute congestion in high request low throughput cases.
- Lower latency overheads
    - With dynamic batching, the latency of processing each request reduces, due to the increased utilization in resources as mentioned above, resulting in batch burst processing and reduced time for scheduling and dispatching individual requests.
- Optimized traffic patterns and lower costs
    - This consolidation of requests helps in routing the consolidated requests within the network at lower cost and better throughput, as dynamic batching at the inference server level can help accumulate a batch of requests before scheduled time for batches to be dispatched, leading to better traffic patterns.
- Improved simultaneous use of multiple devices
    - With the availability of devices specialized for computational tasks, a new layer of hardware comes into the picture which supports the inference server. The use of dynamic batching here helps to deliver more efficient compute cycles where compute devices and servers can simultaneously process a batch of requests.

How to incorporate Dynamic Batching

Dynamic batching is normally at the inference server layer and can be added quite seamlessly depending on the facility of the inference server and its APIs and support for it.  Adding dynamic batching could be just a setting or change in configuration depending on the capability of the inference server.

Dynamic batching is possible to have it work with the typical

- Model API - for data returned by serving one model without any payload but just returns an inference
- Neural API - for data returned by serving a neural network model with weights
- Frame based serving API - with service support based around a vision or a video/audio frame based payload

The ease of incorporating dynamic batching also depends on the support for optimized behavior (hardware support) in the infrastructure of the server.

Similarity-based dynamic batching:

Extending the idea of dynamic batching beyond the domain of inference, one can look at request attributes to decide if a new request can be combined with an existing request. This allows each incoming request to be verified for its content with other requests which are currently in the server awaiting processing and then deciding if the new request can be accumulated with an existing one based on stellar similarity.  This could be for video or audio frames/sequences, or sensor data, or simply
[25/200] Running: Explain greedy decoding vs beam search vs sampling....

🧠 LLM Response:


Greedy decoding, beam search, and sampling are three different methods used in natural language processing models for generating sequences of words.

1. Greedy decoding:
Greedy decoding is a simple and fast method where the model selects the next word based on the highest probability score at each step of the generation process. At each time step, the model generates the most likely next word based on the current context. Greedy decoding does not consider the future consequences of the current choice, and it may not always produce the optimal sequence of words.
2. Beam search:
Beam search is a more sophisticated method than greedy decoding. It is a heuristic search algorithm that maintains a set of the most promising candidate sequences and expands them in parallel at each time step. The algorithm selects the top-k candidates from the previous step and generates all possible next words for each candidate. It then selects the top-k candidates with the highest probability scores and repeats the process until the desired length of the sequence is reached. Beam search is better than greedy decoding at finding the optimal sequence of words, but it may be slower due to the larger memory requirements.
3. Sampling:
Sampling is a randomized method where the model generates words based on their probability scores. The model generates a sequence of words by sampling from the probability distribution of the next word, conditioned on the current context. This method can produce diverse and creative sequences that are not limited to the most likely sequence. However, it can also generate incoherent or incorrect sequences. Sampling can be modified to generate more coherent sequences, such as top-k sampling, where the model only samples from the top-k candidates with the highest probability scores, or nucleus sampling, where the model samples from the smallest set of words that accounts for a certain percentage of the probability mass.

In summary, greedy decoding is the simplest and fastest method, but it may not always produce the optimal sequence. Beam search is a more sophisticated method that considers the future consequences of each choice, but it may be slower due to the higher memory requirements. Sampling is a randomized method that can generate diverse and creative sequences, but it may also produce incoherent or incorrect sequences. The choice of decoding method depends on the application and the desired balance between speed, diversity, and accuracy.
[26/200] Running: What is perplexity in language models and why is it importan...

🧠 LLM Response:


Perplexity is a measure of the uncertainty of a language model when predicting the next word in a sequence. In a logarithmic probability space, lower perplexity indicates that the model is better able to make accurate predictions, as it is less uncertain about the next word.

Perplexity is an important evaluation metric for language models because it allows us to compare the performance of different models by measuring how well they predict the next word in a sequence. By measuring perplexity, we can gain insight into the quality of a language model, as a lower perplexity indicates that the model is better able to understand the underlying data distribution and generate meaningful output.

In other words, perplexity is a measure of how well a language model can understand and generate natural language text. Given a sequence of words, a language model is trained to predict the next word in the sequence. Perplexity measures how consistent the model's predictions are with the actual next word in the sequence. If the model's predictions are consistently accurate, it will have a lower perplexity than a model that is less accurate or less consistent in its predictions.

In summary, perplexity is an important evaluation metric for language models because it provides a quantitative measure of the model's ability to predict the next word in a sequence, which is an essential task in natural language processing applications such as text generation, language translation, and speech recognition.
[27/200] Running: How does mixed precision training benefit model training?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Mixed precision training means using a combination of float16 and float32 data types while training a neural network. The two key functions in NVIDIA TensorRT are capable of performing mixed precision inference, lower Precision Conversion of FP32 and FP16 models, and matrix multiplication. For mixed precision inference, TensorRT accepts a mixed precision network calling it a calibration graph, (where a node's inputs can have different tensor data types) and generates a precision profile in the process. It stores the recommended lower precision data type and the percentage of operations/memory usage for that type in the profile. These recommendations optimize the model for latency and memory bandwidth.

Mixed precision training is similar to mixed precision inference. The difference is that the mixed precision node has FP32 inputs, instead of FP16 inputs. This difference will generate a profile that contains all recommendations as FP16 for the forward pass so that the framework can perform backward and weight update in FP32. NVIDIA's Tensor Cores excel when processing FP16 data types. These operations perform 4x FP16 matrix multiplications and accumulate the results into FP32.

Using mixed precision training while training neural networks brings multiple benefits. Mixed precision is helpful in three main scenarios:

1.Inference Augmentation: Mixed precision training combines the advantages of both FP32 and FP16 data types. FP32 provides higher accuracy but requires more memory bandwidth. Using FP16 ensures statistical precision and has the added benefit of activating the Tensor Cores. The activation of Tensor Cores greatly improves the performance and converges faster than just using FP32.

2.Training acceleration: Using Tensor Cores to process the matrix multiplications lowers the time taken per iteration. In large models that may not have enough memory to fit operators into Tensor Cores, mixed precision training helps to fit more operators in Tensor Cores, resulting in faster training times.

3.Training more with smaller memory footprint: Mixed precision training can lead to converged accuracy results with less memory, allowing for larger batch sizes and/or more models in memory, providing more flexibility during inference.

For more information about NVIDIA's TensorRT, visit the NVIDIA TensorRT product page. For more information about NVIDIA's Deep Learning Institute, visit the NVIDIA DLI product page. For more information about machine learning, visit the NVIDIA developer blog.
