It marks a sequence as true or false. Not sure if it makes sense in your case, because typically this is used in the bidirectional version of the transformer to do sequence labeling (since it can "see" into the future using its bidirectionality), whereas typically the transformer decoder is unidirectional (always looks at the input and the previously decoded tokens).
For a unidirectional version, it would just give the last output token a marked probability.
Probability for the sequence is whether the sequence matches the input given all seq/input pairs (past,future) until i-th and likelihood for i-th token is likelihood of the current true token.
Im not sure, if this in general makes sense in your case, but lets "change it a bit"- you get coarse-grained outputs (you can interpolate between tokens (e.g texts), coarse-grained in terms of multiple labels per token (in case of BERT)) which gives you actual probability (if you normalize that to 1) per output (class, sequence and pred-class, pred-sequence given input and its history, respectively)
E.g. BERT (which has public SOTA on 11 NLP tasks incl generic sequence labeling) uses sentence embeddings as final output and the rest is taken from that vector, that could be for classification, SBERT (sentence-embedding) uses different networks to pre-process and additionally post-process the output- it pulls out unbiased possible/impossible labels from the embeddings, fitness proportionate selection (you get probability for seq/class combos, not individual labels) by using different thresholding methods including a simple "linear" and a piece-wise logistic regression, (actual output sequence often has labels and scores for the labels too, so you can't know if the score is actually needed, but the score is "kinda" required (not all) for fitness-proportionate selection). So the sequence-prediction problem is broken into a toolbox of models (like modifying the last layer - we have a simple linear layer and a piece-wise logistic regression) and you can use/discard and/or modify everything, you want.

"An existing problem in NPL is that most neural models can’t be easily altered and fine-tuned on multiple prob- lems. In our work to make this possible we circuitously showed that n-gram-based languages models can achieve robust performance. It turns out that most of the tasks needed a transformer architecture that had been pre-trained with just bi-gram distributions instead of sentence-level'" - from [Soft Implements and Circuitous Routes to Neural NPL (2020)](https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/3406140)
Im not sure you exactly need it, but I think the last linear layer can be replaced / pre-processed with something more complex like a ml-pipeline depending on your specific needs and you can get coarse-grained outputs from simple tools like linear layers and you get optimal outputs using different NN architectures (especially for complex tasks like PEG Sequence to sequence).
[29/200] Running: Explain embeddings and how they're used in NLP models....

🧠 LLM Response:

Embeddings are a technique for representing categorical variables in a continuous vector space, which allows for the use of linear algebra and other mathematical tools in machine learning models. In NLP, words are often represented as high-dimensional vectors, with each dimension corresponding to a specific word. These word vectors are learned through training a neural network on a large corpus of text data, and they capture semantic and syntactic relationships between words.

For example, a simple word embedding might represent the word "king" as a vector with a large value in the dimension corresponding to the concept of "royalty" and a smaller value in the dimension corresponding to "gender", since a king is typically male. The embedding for the word "queen" would have a similar value in the "royalty" dimension, but a different value in the "gender" dimension. This allows the model to perform operations such as adding the vectors for "king" and "man" to get a vector that is similar to the vector for "prince".

Embeddings are an essential component of many NLP models, such as Word2Vec, GloVe, and BERT. They allow these models to capture complex linguistic patterns and relationships in a way that is efficient and scalable, and they have been instrumental in achieving state-of-the-art performance on a wide range of NLP tasks.

Additionally, embeddings can also be fine-tuned on a specific task, allowing them to capture task-specific information and improve the performance of the model.

Another advantage of embeddings is that they can be used to represent words in multiple languages, allowing for cross-lingual transfer learning. This can be useful for tasks such as machine translation, where a model trained on one language can be fine-tuned on another language with little or no additional data.

Overall, embeddings are a powerful tool for NLP models, allowing them to capture complex linguistic patterns and relationships in a way that is efficient and scalable. They are an essential component of many state-of-the-art NLP models and have been instrumental in achieving impressive performance on a wide range of NLP tasks.
[30/200] Running: Describe the function of the tokenizer's vocabulary....

🧠 LLM Response:

Most word embedding approaches involve using a tokenizer to transform words into numerical vectors. The tokenizer provides this function, as well as a "vocabulary" data structure to keep track of all unique words it has found thus far. The vocabulary mapping in the tokenizer is crucial for the neural network to map between the word/token and the embedded vector.

Tokenizer `integer_encoding` field is a matrix where each row contains the corresponding index of words from their vocabulary, where first word of any document will have 1 and then the second word 2 and so on for vocabulary size. E.g., First word of any document will be integer_encoded as 1, Second word as 2 and so forth depending on the vocabulary size. After the completion of one set of documents, its vocabulary will be stored in the tokenizer vocabulary, then while tokenizing the next set of documents with same or different vocabulary will append it up to the same tokenizer vocabulary.

For Example,
```bash
tokenizer = Tokenizer()
tokenizer.fit_on_texts(['Positive', 'Words', 'and', 'Sentiment'])
print(tokenizer.word_index)
{
"positive": 1,
"words": 2,
"and": 3,
"sentiment": 4,
"negative": 5
}
```
Here, we only have four unique words i.e., `positive`, `words`, `and`, `sentiment`. We use the `Tokenizer` class to implement the tokenizer `fit_on_texts()` function which trains all the words/tokens in the given corpus, and those words are associated with certain unique numbers/indices in `tokenizer.word_index`. We already know the word `negative` is there because it was passed while initializing the tokenizer object but not trained yet.
All the words trained is associated with a unique number in the dictionary pairs which preserves the sequence/order of occurrence.
[31/200] Running: What is causal masking in transformer decoders?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 It is a strategy used to prevent unprotected ethics decoders from generating plausible looking tokens that are causally inconsistent with the input that the model has received. This is vitally important in the case of autoregressive language models that generate text one token at a time as you don’t want them to say false things about the context that they have already seen!

In other words our autoregressive language model has seen ‘my dog is ‘ and on the next step we don’t want it to say ‘blue’, accidentally misleading us into thinking that the dog is blue, when in fact that observation had not been mentioned. To prevent this from happening we use a causally masked embeddings matrix that simply shields the previous tokens to the current one from influencing the model at the predictive step.

Causal masking means that a dot product contained in the multiplication of queries with keys, transformed by values, within the self attention mechanism will mask out any key that is from a different position to the queries.

Essentially restricts connections from things where the currently decoded time-step is before the sequence we are decoding. Masquerading those token embeddings in such a way that they can’t influence the current decoding step but permit them to influence subsequent decoding process. The respective position mask would look something like this:

But what about the query that we should see in the diagonal? The reason why it’s not contains is because we use padding tokens to make the input data the same length as maximum input sequence. The padding tokens receive attention scores but we ultimately ignore them, so we can ignore them as well in the masking procedure too.

There’s a very small cost associated with causal masking, for an input of length 512 and batch size of 32 the cost would be approximately 70MB, which may be neglected considering that modern transformer models have practically no upper bound on their memory consumption.

However I do love its elegance, it seems like a minor detail but it’s particularly important, it’s that safety mechanism that ensures that the model won’t lie to you and lead you to believe that things are other than they truly are.

There’s also the alternative of bidirectional self-attention, where all input embeddings influence the modeling at each step, that might be better suited for some supervised tasks where the nature of the context is purely static. But for autoregressive models, that are quite prone to invent things as they go along, causal masking is the way to go!

The task could be something as simple as language modeling. So the objective is to predict probability distribution over next token. Maybe the input is my dog and we would like to predict what comes next. Causal masking would just impede the attention weights to influence each other in a causally inconsistent manner.

A stack of several causally masked transformer decoders can form an autoregressive language model! One whose creativity is limited solely within the confines of the input text that they have received.

Causal masking can also be applied to any model that employs causality within itsattentional network and can even be used to a broad family of problems, such as temporal and spatio-temporal sequence modeling, for more details check out LongFormer: The Long-Document Transformer.

While their memories can span up to 4096 tokens, larger documents will require the attention mask to be shifted to reflect longer sequences. By concatenating rows of the causal mask we can obtain the desired attention pattern. It should be noted, however, that the zero-ing out of elements within the attention mask leads to a contraction in the computational graph, which can soon lead to enormous memory requirements. Longformer utilizes a multi-query attention mechanism to keep reduced its memory footprint even when causally masking is ensued.

But one thing that I’ve seen that is used to reach very long range sequence dependencies, that can span even up to 16k tokens, is recursively applying causally masked transformers as a type of recursion mechanism. The idea is for the transformer layers to not only generate the next token but also the next token embedding, recursively. This leads to the longer conntections that were previously unreachable without massive amounts of computation and memory! This sounds like a truly powerful and interesting way to perform distributed attention.

Causal masking, as well as self-attention, has also been applied to the problem of causally-inconsistent translations, namely those referring to things that were not seen in the
[32/200] Running: How does gradient checkpointing reduce memory usage?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Assume X a input to layer (i-1) and A a activation of layer i. Layer i stores (X, A, gradient_wrt_A, gradient_wrt_X). Gradient checkpointing stores (on demand) only (X, gradient_wrt_X). Before layer i updates its weights, the missing A and gradient_wrt_A is recomputed by looking from the X and X_gradient of layer i and A of layer i-1. It saves half of the memory because the activation maps are recomputed instead of storing them.

A couple of further clarifications:

1. Sometimes the memory used for gradient checkpointing can be *more* than the memory used when not using gradient checkpointing. This is because gradient checkpointing requires computing forward passes multiple times, so for a single mini-batch it might require more memory. The usual mitigation here is to either increase the mini-batch size (which requires more memory), or to use a larger compute device (which also requires more memory).
2. You mentioned A, X, dA and dX for layer i, but that's not typical. Instead, people usually just store the activations A for all layers and the gradients with respect to their weights dW for all layers. (I have an earlier answer that explains the distinction between dA and dW: How is dA (the derivative of activation) used in ML?) It's possible that some combinations of frameworks and optimizers could cause someone to store those extra variables, but I think it would be unusual.
3. Gradient checkpointing is not always about saving memory, sometimes it's used to *avoid* an out-of-memory error, and the strategy is about discarding some variables that you can afford to recompute. Memory savings is a nice side effect, but for very deep networks it's possible that the recomputation time (and the need for more compute capacity) might not be worth the memory savings.
4. I don't recall when or where I learned what "gradient checkpointing" meant, but I'm pretty sure it comes from the practice of computing gradients "on demand". I have a slide+captions about how this works in the context of RNNs at Week 7 Applications of Deep Learning -> Recurrent Neural Networks -> Modeling Long Ranges of Time. That explains the concept, although it doesn't use the term "gradient checkpointing".

Those are all minor points though. Hope they add more information for readers!
[33/200] Running: Explain the difference between encoder-decoder and decoder-o...

🧠 LLM Response:


Encoder-decoder models and decoder-only models are two types of architectures used in natural language processing (NLP) for various tasks such as machine translation, summarization, and text generation.

Encoder-Decoder Models:
Encoder-decoder models consist of two separate components: an encoder and a decoder. The encoder takes an input sequence and encodes it into a fixed-length vector representation, which captures the semantic meaning of the input sequence. The decoder then takes this fixed-length vector and generates an output sequence.

Encoder-decoder models are often used in tasks such as machine translation, where the input sequence is a sentence in one language, and the output sequence is the corresponding sentence in another language. In this case, the encoder encodes the input sentence into a fixed-length vector, and the decoder generates the output sentence in the target language.

Decoder-Only Models:
Decoder-only models, on the other hand, consist of only a decoder component. These models generate output sequences without an explicit input sequence. Instead, the model generates the output sequence one token at a time, conditioned on the previous tokens it has generated.

Decoder-only models are often used in tasks such as text generation, where the goal is to generate a sequence of text without any explicit input sequence. For example, the model could be used to generate a news article or a product description.

The main difference between encoder-decoder and decoder-only models is that encoder-decoder models have an explicit input sequence, and the output sequence is generated based on this input sequence. In contrast, decoder-only models do not have an explicit input sequence and generate the output sequence based on the previous tokens it has generated.

Encoder-decoder models are better suited for tasks that require understanding the semantic meaning of an input sequence, such as machine translation or summarization, while decoder-only models are better suited for tasks that involve generating text without an explicit input sequence, such as text generation.

Overall, encoder-decoder and decoder-only models are two distinct architectures used in NLP tasks, and the choice of architecture depends on the specific task and the desired outcome.
[34/200] Running: What is zero-shot vs few-shot vs fine-tuned inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:

=====================================================

Briefly put, zero-shot inference, few-shot inference, and fine-tuned inference refer
to conceptually different learning processes to use or customize a pretrained language
model to a given task. Although we tend to focus on unsupervised learning,
training machine learning models in general still often needs supervised learning.
This is because inferred knowledge from supervised data is typically more concrete
compared to unsupervised learning, and thus, models trained with supervised
data are often also more interpretable and reliable in practice.

Zero-shot inference
--------------------

Zero-shot inference is a rather intuitive concept. It refers to the idea that
you can feed any text data to a pretrained model to learn semantic
representations so that the similarity of such representations can be measured
to infer/rate the similarities or differences among the pieces of data.

This is the most 'computer literate' among the later two inference processes, since
the process requires no coding or model modification at all. After the learning
phase, only the LM can be fed with a query template that consists of placeholder
text (in square brackets) and a query to be filled in that place.

The **skeleton template** is a sentece template that encodes the task. For example,
'An ideal system does not *<strong>[NEGATIVE]</strong>* the creativity of *<strong>[TARGET]</strong>'
is a skeleton template for pairwise ranking, where the embedding of **NEGATIVE** word is
inverse weighted against the embedding of **TARGET** word to rank a pair of words. Note
that this skeleton template consists of a query placeholder as in '[TARGET]'.

To use zero-shot inference, you can simply 'fill in' the placeholder in the
sentence template that acts like a template for the actual query, so that it looks like
as follows:

.. code:: ipython3

    query = model.generate({'input_dict': {'query_1': f"An ideal system does not [NEGATIVE] the creativity of {pair[0]} | query_2: An ideal system does not [NEGATIVE] the creativity of {pair[1]} }"}})

For more details, please refer to :ref:`quickstart paragraphs<pairwise-template>`.

Few-shot inference
-------------------

Few-shot inference is also a rather intuitive concept: We can supervise the model by
few in-contect examples (data if available). Few-shot inference is used when you
have very few labeled/annotated data to learn from. This is called **boostrap learning**
as opposed to bootstrapping, which starts from training with all identified data.

Few-shot inference differs from zero-shot inference in the sense that it exploits
 some label/annotation data to learn from it. The examples thus present a bias for
 learning. For example, you can include multiple positively contrastive and negative
 contrastive examples to a query skeleton to increase the chance of success to
 choose a correct contrastive example from a list of relevant examples:

.. code:: ipython3

    pos_text_template_1 = '<strong>[POSITIVE_1]</strong> is a valid example for a *<strong>[INSTANCE]</strong>* of */categories/<strong>[CATEGORY]</strong>.*'

    pos_text_template_2 = '<strong>[POSITIVE_2]</strong> is another valid example of $<\\strong>[INSTANCE]</\\strong>$ from */categories/<strong>[CATEGORY]</strong>.*'

    pos_text_template_3 = '<strong>[POSITIVE_3]</strong> is a good demonstration for */categories/<strong>[CATEGORY]</strong>.*'

    neg_text_template_1 = '<strong>[NEGATIVE_1]</strong> is an **invalid** example for a */categories//<strong>[CATEGORY]</strong> instance.*'

    neg_text_template_2 = '<strong>[NEGATIVE_2]</strong> is an **invalid** example for *<strong>[INSTANCE]</strong>* of */categories/<strong>[C
[35/200] Running: What are prompt templates and why do they matter?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Prompt templates are simply ways of referring to text input prompts, which are a common way to help natural language AI models like ChatGPT to generate responses. They might be as simple as a single word "story", a phrase like "explain quantum physics in simple terms" or a full paragraph describing a complex situation.

The wording of prompts can make a big difference in many ways, from setting the correct context to avoiding inappropriate responses. They also matter because they can be used to control the amount and type of output from a model, shaping both the volume and usefulness of what is generated.

One study looking at text classification found that a prompt template phrased one way had a 70% accurate outcome versus a 90% outcome with a different phrasing. This isn't to suggest that ChatGPT or similar models are 90% accurate on the task of text classification unless you use the right prompt template, but it does show the degree of influence that well-worded prompts can have on the AI's functioning.

Other studies found that using more detailed or contextualized prompt templates can help produce superior results in a wide variety of tasks, including textual summarization, question generation and language translation.

The reason for this is that the quality and type of training data that these machine learning algorithms rely on has inherent limitations and can't cover every conceivable situation. Human language users rely on the tons of context we pick up all around us, from the situations we find ourselves in, to subtle nuances we understand from other people's expressions or from what they're doing with their hands when they're talking.

Artificial intelligence doesn't have the same sensory clues that we do, and while it can sometimes extract useful extra information from images or the likes, in many cases developers need to rely on a slower and somewhat less precise method: including additional details in the text prompt.

For people who have used AI tools like ChatGPT, responding to a prompt with lengthy context description might seem strange: don't they already get it from the input I gave them? And if the model is capable of parsing complex intent from that input, why does it need a lot of framing context beforehand?

In some cases, it shouldn't – but in others the prompt template may be acting more like a cue, telling the AI that not only should it answer a question, but give context to the answer. For example, a prompt "Explain the Code of Hammurabi to children in under 100 words with no abstruse vocabulary" produces results like this:

Hammurabi's Code was an old set of rules. It said who did wrong, and what the punishment was. For example, if a builder made a house fall down, they could be put to death. Some of these rules were fair, but not all of them.

Compare that to this prompt:

Translate Hammurabi's Code to simple language for 5th grade children to understand and remember.

This generates a very different result:

Hammurabi's Code was a list of very important rules that Babylonians had to follow. It was made by King Hammurabi in about 1770BC. Some of the rules were about crime and what happened to criminals, like if a doctor made a patient sick, they had to look after the sick person for free until they got well.

Even if the baseline understanding of the AI might be the same, it's clear that a prompt template with some contextual framing can make a big difference in the outcome.

This principle holds true in a number of contexts, as we'll see below.

## Useful framing in question/answer interactions

Providing a well-framed question to an AI can help get around some of the inherent limitations of its understanding of the world. For example, AI models like ChatGPT aren't truly sentient beings or even conscious automatons: they learn from the data they have been trained on and can't actually experience the world outside the examples shown to them.

To a human being, the first question "Why should I trust a machine learning model that can't understand the events unfolding in the moment or fully comprehend complex concepts?" probably holds more weight than "Is it true that you can't understand the world outside your training and don't fully comprehend complex concepts?" The latter is a yes/no question where the former isn't.

The way you frame the prompt affects the way the AI interacts with you, subtly suggesting what type of
