🧠 LLM Response:


Leaderboard does not mention MoE as a submission strategy. I found no information about MoE in 108th progress report. Maybe I missed it.

Mixture of Experts (MoE) is a fairly old but proven concept that combines multiple models to improve results and make predictions that adapt to the data. Given observations of an input pattern and output pair { x, y }, we learn a set of N models ${h}_{i}(·)$ associated with a gate network or advisor layer f(x):

$y(x)=\sum _{i=1}^{N}{f}_{i}(x){h}_{i}(x)$

With ${f}_{i}(x)$ being the non-linearity, we'll continue with the following formulation using soft-max which represents probabilities:

$y(x)=\frac{\sum _{i=1}^{N}{e}^{{f}_{i}(x)}{h}_{i}(x)}{\sum _{i=1}^{N}{e}^{{f}_{i}(x)}}$

Special considerations:

1. Find a trade-off for both f(x) and h([x,task_id]).
2. Every model receives the same input x.
3. All f_[i] needs to be reduced to a single value.
4. The gate network may also learn to focus on different subsets of the input space.

The challenge here is how the weights of N experts are adjusted. Extensive training sessions and incremental iterations are required to evaluate the model and its performance.

This image summarizes how Mixture of Experts architecture adapt the weights and combines multiple models.

About Ensemble of Experts:
Just as ensemble models use multiple (hundreds, thousands) weak models to get accurate results. MoE uses the same concept but it focuses on using multiple accurate models.

Note:
1. This is a text adaptation from my latest personal post on MoE.
2. Mauricio Alvarez does a very good job explaining MoE and also other approaches including expert-gate variables and its differences with ensemble.
3. I missed the opportunity to mention this earlier in the community-pilot forum. hence the ticket to share my thoughts.

Disclaimer: Apart from the competition, I am passionate about neural networks and data science. Therefore, I decided to write about the concept and share my thoughts of what is not mentioned so far.

