This ambiguity makes the topic particularly interesting to write about as well as an exciting opportunity to contribute to the ongoing discussion about these issues. The following presents an opinionated summary of the problem, highlighting the most prominent issues while also avoiding easy answers or generalizations.

Generative adversarial networks (GANs) [1] are currently the most popular models associated with this discussion. Other models such as variational autoencoders (VAEs) or flow-based models might belong to the same category, but they are not discussed in here for simplicity but also because they are generally perceived as less problematic.

Before jumping into it, I want to be clear that I have no academic background in ethics, and this post in no way claims to be an exhaustive discussion on the subject. The focuses of the included references, as well as my own, are not on ethics, but rather the technical aspects of generative models. It is important to note, however, that these same technical aspects often have ethical implications, and thus technically-focused discussions become relevant to the ethical discussion [2].

# Generative models

Generative models are a category of machine learning models that learn the probability distributions of large datasets, allowing them to randomly sample new data. They’re commonly used to generate images, music, or even text, although they can be applied to any type of data, such as audio, text, graphs, or even MRI images.

Generative models can be divided into two categories: likelihood models and implicit models [3]. Likelihood models, also known as energy-based models or density models, estimate the direct joint probability of the observed data through a big mathematical formula (which is not important here). Implicit models, including GANs, learn to sample from this distribution without explicitly estimating its probability density.

# Naming conventions

Before discussing ethical concerns, it’s important to clear up a minor terminological issue. Some authors use specific terms for specific types of generative models. For instance, Adlam et al. [4] use the term “prestidigitation” to refer to generative models that create entirely new content, and the term “impersonation” to refer to models that act as the mouthpiece for already existing content, such as deepfakes, although they never specifically use this term.

Others, such as Gurumurthy [5], use “impersonation” as a synonym for generative models that mimic existing content, but do not use “prestidigitation.” The term “deepfakes” is often used by the general media, although there are attempts to define it clearly: one recent widely discussed deepfake was a staged video of Tom Cruise [6].

In this post, I use “prestidigitation” to refer to the general act of generating data which does not necessarily need to represent anything in the real world. I use “impersonation” to refer to those models that purport to represent specific individuals.

