Generative models are fascinating precisely because they can be used for prestidigitation. Arguably, perturbations of the data is achieved by any model and are therefore not part of the problem.

2006: the first generative image model revolutionizes the 250 year-old field of computer graphics by bringing together machine learning in a very tangible way. Arguably, rendering is not part of the ethical discussion here, but the sheer speed of it was, at that moment, unheard of.

2014: GANs allow us to train generative models without too much knowledge about the underlying math. This was a significant breakthrough, as until that point, generative models were often hard to get working, mostly because they involved some heavy-duty sampling strategies from a tricky equation. Arguably, the generation of images has not changed much since that point from the practical perspective, but things have become way easier.

