The way transformers process multilingual text is by having the ability to project text to an embedding space where it can have higher level representation, regardless of language, this allows transformers can be trained on multiple languages at once and still be able to understand and process the text well. Most transformers use relaxing penalization methods to tokenize text from different languages so its easier to convert and learn from.

The original Transformer used in 2017 was only trained for English, but in 2018, people realized that making the model multilingual would greatly increase its ability to learn and process information from a variety of languages and cultures, leading to the advent of the “Multilingual BERT” (M-BERT), which can process nearly 104 languages with no modification to the model itself.

M-BERT uses a subword tokenizer called WordPiece, which can split words into subwords to help further break down and map words to their embedding space. The WordPiece algorithm uses likely survival of subword segments to generate a sub-token vocabulary, which can account for prevalent subword segments for various languages. By doing this, M-BERT can learn the same algorithm for multiple languages, making it highly applicable and adaptable.
