[78/200] Running: What are the differences between HPU and GPU tensors?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In Deep Learning, some tasks call for processing 3D data such as CT-Scans, whereas others call for 2D data such as Images. Each of these datatypes call for different computation methods, hence the introduction of 3D and 2D tensors.
Computations with these tensors can be done on both the CPU and GPU. When working using the CPU, the inputs are naturally considered HPU tensors (Host Processing Unit) tensors and when working in the GPU, the inputs are naturally considered GPU tensors.

HPU tensors are managed by the deep learning library (PyTorch/Tensorflow) on the host machine, whereas, GPU tensors are managed by the deep learning library on the GPU itself. When working on the host machine, there are numerous CPU cores available to manage these computations and data, therefore, computations on the CPU are generally slower than the GPU due to the number of cores available for computations.

GPU tensors in contrast have faster cores and theoretically are made for tensor (multi-dimensional matrix) computations. The number of GPU cores generally outnumber CPU cores. Therefore, in deep learning, GPU tensors are generally faster than HPU tensors for multi-dimensional matrix computations i.e. matrix multiplication, summations.
Furthermore, GPU cards such as Nvidia have dedicated logic and architecture tailored for fast tensor matrix operations as well as parallel processing, which makes them a better option for deep learning tasks as well as other tasks that require parallel processing on multi-dimensional arrays.

In summary, depending on the deep learning task at hand, use GPU tensors if you are concerned about speed as they will perform faster than HPU tensors and utilize multi-dimensional matrix operations. However, HPU tensors are sufficient for most deep learning tasks and even some heavy ones (such large language models), one of the top reasons is the availability of the CPUs. More importantly, in many cloud services and even in some recent computers, the cpu is not distint from the gpu motherboard, therefore, the use case restricts to how the deep learning framework in concern manages tensors.

EstebanGonzalezSosa
Software Engineer at Cisco Meraki

I would add here the main difference which is types of computations and tasks for which we can use GPUs vs CPUs. GPUs are designed to handle parallel computations, which are better for transforming large amounts of data, low-level mathematical computations and most machine learning applications. On the other hand, CPUs are better for serial based instructions and computations, which is more application based work.

Examples:

* You have an algorithm that needs to calculate large matrix multiplications on huge datasets, where each number passed to the operation is independent of the other, you would have better performance with GPUs.
* You have an application that needs to process data and return results in a specific order where each instruction is dependent on the previous one, you would use a CPU.

Lastly, I love the comment made by @YamaçKarasu, which I would like to expand on. There are other components apart from GPUs and CPUs, that can perform tasks better than the first two. For example, FPGAs can be used for physical layer processing on radio systems, or TPUs can be used for machine learning and neural network acceleration. Decisions need to be made not only by current capabilities but also the future growth of the system.

