[85/200] Running: Explain the principle behind rotary embeddings....

🧠 LLM Response:


Rotary embeddings is a method of mapping position and velocity information from physical 2D or 3D space into a high-dimensional space in such a way that the rotational symmetries of that space are preserved. This is achieved by storing a complex sinusoidal basis for the coordinates, where the sine and cosine components are treated separately and independently for each dimension.

At its core, rotary embeddings work by taking a position or velocity vector in physical space, and converting it into a set of complex numbers. Each component of the vector is transformed into a complex sinusoidal oscillation with a frequency specific to that dimension. The resulting complex embedding vector can then be used as input to a neural network or other machine learning model.

The key advantage of rotary embeddings is that they preserve the rotational symmetries of the physical space being embedded. This means that the same pattern should be recognized regardless of the orientation of the input vector, making the resulting embeddings more robust to changes in position or orientation. This has been shown to be particularly useful in tasks such as object detection and tracking, where the network needs to be able to recognize the same object in different orientations.

Overall, rotary embeddings provide a powerful tool for mapping physical space into high-dimensional space in a way that preserves the symmetries of the physical world. This has the potential to improve the performance of machine learning models in a wide range of applications.
