[91/200] Running: How are fused attention layers implemented?...

🧠 LLM Response:

============================================

Fused attention layers are implemented as a single layer, with the computation
of several attention mechanisms (e.g. Additive, Multiplicative, or Dot) at
different levels of the layers determined by parameters and “gates” that can
be learned via backpropagation.

The Architecture
================

The architecture of our fused attention layer is shown in the following transposed
listing which is meant to show the flow of computation in the forward pass.
The variable `k` denotes the number of different attention levels in the layer,
`d` is the size of the hidden states, and `s` is the size of the auxiliary
features.

.. highlight:: python

.. code-block:: python

   stateful = MultihopAttentionLayers (
         name="multi_hop",
         W_trainable=True,  # should be True for optimization
         useAdditiveAttention=True,
         useDotAttention=True,
         useMultiplicativeAttention=True,
         additiveAttentionCeilingLayerSize=1000,  # arbitrary for example only
         additiveAttentionRandomMtxSizes=[50, 75, 125],  # for example only
         dotAttentionLogitsScalingFactor=1.0,
         layerSizes=[d, s, s, s, s]
   )(inputSource, inputAuxiliary)

The ``UseAdditiveAttention``, ``UseDotAttention``, and ``UseMultiplicativeAttention``
parameters determine which attention mechanisms to include in the layer.  Note
that different mechanisms can be combined at different layers via the appropriate
use of the ``layerSizes`` parameter.

The Additive attention implementation is shown below as a separate listing which
