[94/200] Running: What is a sequence-to-sequence model?...

🧠 LLM Response:


## Model Overview

The Sequence-to-Sequence model (Seq2Seq model) is a type of neural network architecture used primarily in natural language processing (NLP) tasks such as:

- Machine Translation
- Text Summarization
- Sentiment Analysis
- Speech Recognition

The model is called a Sequence-to-Sequence model because it maps an input sequence to an output sequence.

![2-seq2seq-archdiagram.png](/content/images/2019/02/2-seq2seq-archdiagram.png)

At a high level, the architecture is composed of two main components:

1. Encoder
2. Decoder

**Encoder**

The encoder encodes the input sequence into a fixed-length context vector and is fed to the decoder as a starting point.

**Decoder**

The decoder takes generates the output sequence, one timestep at a time. More specifically, at each timestep $t$, the decoder receives an input, which is the token at timestep $t – 1$ (i.e. the output at the previous timestep), and generates an output token (typically performed through a softmax over the output probability distribution).


What is a sequence-to-sequence model with attention?

In the basic form of Seq2Seq model described above, the encoder always encodes inputs into a fixed-length context vector, regardless of the length of the input sequence. Hence, for long sequences, certain details can be lost when mapping long input sequences to a fixed-length context vector.

To alleviate such issues, Bahdanau et. al of Google proposed [attention mechanism](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.0473.pdf), which dynamically assigns more/less attention to aspects of the input sequence, when generating each token.

Note: Before Attention, what we did was to feed in the encoder's context vector into the start of every decoder sequence (one-timestep-at-a-time), irrespective of the length of the decoder sequence. With Attention, instead of feeding one context vector, we feed in a sequence of context vectors - one for each time-step in the decoder's sequence. The network dynamically learns to choose which ones to weight - more/less attention to certain vectors.

## Encoder-Decoder Attention Architecture

![3-seq2seq-attention-archdiagram.png](/content/images/2019/02/3-seq2seq-attention-archdiagram.png)

The figure above shows an Encoder-Decoder with attention mechanism. Here, at each timestep $t$ of the decoder, the decoder computes a score $e_{ij}$, for each input vector $i$ in the encoder’s sequence. The score $e_{ij}$ is an indication of the relevance of input vector $i$ with respect to the current decoder timestep $j$. This score is computed as:

$$
e_{ij} \equiv \text{softmax} ( \mathbf{v}_a^T \tanh ( \mathbf{W}_1 \mathbf{h}_i + \mathbf{W}_2 \mathbf{s}_j + \mathbf{b}_a ))
$$

where the input vector $i$’s annotations are a part of the encoder's RNN hidden state, $\mathbf{h}$.

The score $e_{ij}$ is computed for each element in the input sequence. The final context or attention vector $\mathbf{c}$ is calculated with a weightage according to the amount of attention assigned to each of the input vectors.

$$
\mathbf{c}_j = \sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{ij}\mathbf{h}_i
$$

where, $\mathbf{h}_i$ is  the hidden state of an encoder at time step $i$, $n$ is the number of hiddenunits of an encoder, and $a_{ij}$ is attention score that we calculated previously.

The intention here is to create a single annotated vector $\mathbf{c}_j$ that is used as additional input, combined with $s\_t$ for decoding at the time step $j$.

Now there are several different waysBAHDANAU2014 to compute $c\_t$, most notable of which is the {\it Bahdanau
