In infilling (corrupted text multi-turn chatting), why do we need the tokenizer.mask_token?

We use `pad_token_id` to pad a sequence to a certain length. In generation, this is useful when you want to limit the ouput sequence length without truncating the turther prompt part.
It can also be useful for parallel decoding.
For infilling, you should not use the mask token, instead, you replace the masked span with the `<mask>` token.
The mask token, intended to represent certain spans to predict, will be horrible for learning the relationship between certain words.
In infilling, we use the mask token to show the corrupted words/spans for the model to predict.
[96/200] Running: Explain the concept of auto-regressive generation....

🧠 LLM Response:
 What are its advantages and disadvantages compared to other generative methods?

Auto-regressive generation is a method of generative modeling in which a model generates data by predicting the next value based on the previous values. This is in contrast to other generative methods such as Variational Autoencoder (VAE) or Generative Adversarial Networks (GAN) which generate data all at once.

The key advantage of auto-regressive generation is that it can generate high-quality samples, especially for sequential data such as natural language, music, and speech. The reason for this is that the model is trained to predict the next value based on the previous values, which allows it to capture the dependencies and patterns in the data more accurately. Additionally, the auto-regressive approach allows for generating data incrementally, which can be useful in situations where generating data all at once is not feasible or desirable.

On the other hand, the auto-regressive approach also has some disadvantages. One of the main disadvantages is that it can be computationally expensive, especially for high-dimensional data. This is because the model has to make a prediction for each value in the data, which can require a large number of forward passes through the model. Additionally, the auto-regressive approach can be more difficult to train compared to other generative methods, as it requires careful tuning of the optimization algorithm and the model architecture.

Another disadvantage of auto-regressive generation is that it can be difficult to ensure diversity in the generated samples. This is because the model is trained to predict the next value based on the previous values, which can lead to repetitive patterns in the generated data. To address this issue, techniques such as top-k sampling and nucleus sampling can be used to introduce randomness and diversity in the generated samples.

In summary, auto-regressive generation is a powerful generative method that can produce high-quality samples for sequential data, but it has some disadvantages such as computational expense, difficulty in training, and limited diversity in the generated samples. Therefore, it is important to carefully consider the advantages and disadvantages of this method before using it in a particular application.
