Top-p sampling is a type of nucleus sampling where instead of sampling from the entire distribution, we sample from the smallest subset of top-p mass where p is the probability of selection. Nucleus sampling is just sampling from the smallest set of top-k elements. In certain applications, top-p sampling works better than nucleus sampling and vice versa. One might confuse top-k sampling with top-p sampling with k ≈ p. But top-k sampling will contain elements with probabilities completely contributed by k elements, whereas top-p sampling might contain probabilities from other elements as well. Top-k sampling with k = n ensures that the distribution is still the same. But in top-p sampling, we don’t have such a guarantee. Every element in top-p sampling might contribute to the probabilities differently. It depends on our application to use top-p or nucleus. It’s also better to use multiple types of sampling to get the best prediction in some applications. So, overall, there is no single rule for any applications where one works better than the other. It depends on the application.

