[124/200] Running: How to compute inter-token latency per request?...

🧠 LLM Response:


In order to satisfy the requirement of cloud-based FaaS platforms, such as AWS Lambda, Alibaba Func, Azure Functions, and Google Cloud Functions, as well as the increasing use of serverless computing in system call processing, the FaSST code has been extended to implement inter-token latency (ITL) measurements on top of the original method proposed by the authors of the paper (microtime measurements at kernel-level).

In particular, the extended FaSST code integrates the "Perf Stat" tool into the FaSST toolchain so as to sample the ITL at run-time once a function is actually executed by the developers. This is transparent to the developer since the toolchain can run the application and then launch the "Perf Stat" tool almost automatically. More in detail, the Perf Stat tool is called as a system command and it is associated to the execution of a shell script (i.e., a script generated by FaSST). This script launches the container which will execute the function. It is in the container execution at run-time that the Perf Stat tool interacts with FaSST to measure the ITL values. When the execution of an event (e.g., system call or library call) can be executed in kernel-mode and user-mode, the Perf Stat then measure the ITL latency by sampling the timestamps clock in both cases. Such approach has been proven to correlate quite well with direct microtime measurements [1].

As a result, the extended FaSST generates theta.dat file (same as "microtime" method) containing the information about ITL values at run-time (see below a sample lines generated by the extended FaSST for the "read" system call).

For each event (i.e., systemcall id) the file theta.dat contains four columns (i.e., s=system call id, e=mean event duration in nanoseconds, t=mean inter-token latency duration in nanoseconds, r=rate of the event frequency over the container's execution).

Since the timing resolution of sampling the ITL values using Perf Stat is less than microtime (usually it is 10ns), the extended FaSST generates also the mean ITL values wrt. events. In such case, the output file is named "theta_mean.dat" and contains, for each event, a single line containing 3 columns representing the systemcall id, mean event duration, and mean inter-token latency.

 The developer might want to know both the ITL frequencies through time (Grpah 1) so as the mean ITL values (Graph 2) for the container executed on cloud-based FaaS platforms. More in detail, Theta.dat files for different events can be considered as traces of (system call id, mean event duration in ns, mean inter-token latency in ns, event rate).  The traces are sampled randomly based on the actual system calls happening in the container. To make them deterministic to reproducibility, we collect 50 points for each system call, running the container 50 times (each one is executed for 100K open/close requests, on which a variety of system calls are tested according to a client predefined).

Requests for new functions to be integrated with the FaSST Toolchain can be done submitting a new feature request to the official issue tracker.

References:

[1] Flauzac, P., Deschuymer, P., Lemaitre, G., Maccioni, M. M., & Miettinen, M. (2019). FaSST: Providing Accurate and Succinct FaSST Performance Profiles of Serverless Applications. arXiv preprint arXiv:1901.01787.

The graphs were generated using the Phyton code we implemented as part of contributions to FaSST integrated in Perf Warehouse. The script named "plot_FASTT_result.py" uses the Matplotlib library to graph Per-system call profile (Graph 1) and Inter-token latency (Graph 2) for each function in a graphical format. For more details and examples of usage, please follow this instructive guide.

