[136/200] Running: How does `max_new_tokens` differ from `max_length`?...

🧠 LLM Response:


The `max_new_tokens` parameter controls the maximum number of tokens the model is allowed to generate at *each step* during completion of a prompt.

The `max_length` parameter controls the maximum length of the total sequence to be generated after the completion.

To better understand this, with `max_new_tokens` = 40 and a prompt length of 10 tokens, the model will generate up to 30 more tokens after the prompt (10 + 30 = 40).

Furthermore, suppose you have set a `max_length` = 50; thus, the model's output will be cut short after the first 50 tokens. In this case, if the model generated 44 tokens during completion of the prompt, only the first 40 will be returned, while the remaining 4 will be discarded. Hence, using `max_new_tokens` ensures that all generated tokens are returned.
[137/200] Running: Explain the difference between loss and perplexity....

🧠 LLM Response:


Perplexity is the measure of how well a probability distribution or probability model predicts a sample. It is defined as 2 to the power of cross-entropy for the distribution. Lower perplexity indicates better predictive ability.

Loss, on the other hand, is a measure of the difference between the predicted value and the actual value. It is a scalar value and is used to train machine learning models by comparing the predicted output with the actual output and trying to minimize this difference. Different types of loss functions are used for different types of models, such as mean squared error for regression, and categorical crossentropy for classification.

In summary, perplexity is a measure of how well a probability distribution predicts a sample, while loss is a measure of the difference between the predicted and actual values.
