What are attention heads and how do they specialize? Bastings et al. find that self-attention heads in Transformer models act as feature detectors by attending to specific aspects of the input: a given head may be responsible for detecting negation, a logical conjunction, possessive nouns, etc. They explore ways to identify specific roles for heads, and propose Diagnostic Classification (DiagClass), which determines specialization by training binary classifiers to test whether a member of a pair of sentences has a particular property (e.g. whether one sentence entails the other). Their experiments. results show that the classifiers’ accuracy improve over the course of training, suggesting that heads progressively specialize.

OVERVIEW:

> due to the overparameterization of neural language models, and due to the fact we have no principled way of understanding what would constitute a set of minimal, but rich features for natural language inference, we let the models themselves learn what features to use.

What aspects of natural language inference should ML models learn? Answering this question is difficult without a clear understanding of the features that are relevant for a given NLI task; moreover, some features may be difficult to determine a priori. Overparameterized Transformer models can learn such features from raw input, avoiding the need to specify them beforehand.

Specifically, the authors investigate whether heads in Transformer self-attention layers learn syntax- and semantics-driven features without explicit supervision, and whether these features correspond to natural language properties like word senses and negation. They find that some attention heads specialize over the course of training, so that the attention patterns of different heads can be directly tied to specific linguistic abilities; they argue heads thus effectively learn feature detectors for specific aspects of natural language.

The authors emphasize the importance of understanding what features models learn and what linguistic properties they attend to - without this understanding, they claim predictive power may prove unreliable. Keep reading to learn more about the authors’ exploration of what natural language features different self-attention heads encode and how these rules are learned.

