[140/200] Running: How to ensure reproducible benchmarking?...

🧠 LLM Response:

=========================================

Reproducibility of benchmark results is one of the key problems in
performance evaluation. The relevant goal is to ensure that the
benchmark results, when recreated on the same or another system,
remain the same. It is important to distinguish reproducibility of a
result from getting the same "numbers", arguably easy, and ensuring
that the details of the execution are guaranteed to be preserved.

Without well-planned measures, execution details might differ from run
to run. Anything from the state of other processes running on the
same host, to the content of the system cache, to the disturbance by
external factors, will lead to detectable variations in the final
results. Control of reproducibility improves the measurement process
by the following constraints.

- **statistical reliability**: ensure that the results are not the
  effect of an occasional fluctuation, but represent rather a stable
  behavior pattern of the system under evaluation.

- **experimental rigor**: examine the data to find out if it complies
  with reasonable prior expectations, or if it is an anomaly caused
  by uncontrolled variables.

- **test adequacy**: build confidence in the test process, ensuring
  it adequately measures what we intend to measure.

How to ensure reproducible benchmarking
--------------------------------------

A reproducible result must be repeatable. While it can be difficult
to go further and are able to reproduce previous results on a
different machine and in a different research environment, any result
worth publishing should be repeatable on the exact same machine and
under the exact same experimental conditions. Several factors might
affect repeatability: state of the system, environmental conditions,
instrumentation flaws, setup errors or system unpredictable
effects.

Here's a review of important questions to ask about both
pre-execution and during-execution environments for repeating an
experiment.

Managing pre-execution environments
-----------------------------------

- The initial boot should not be interrupted by external interventions
- No manpages, utilities or packages should be installed during the
  test execution.

- **`isc-dhcp-server <http://www.isc.org/software/dhcp>`__
  and ``named`` should be uninstalled since it would interfere with
  NTP service or make system settings unpredictable when the
  network settings are distributed

- System daemons such as BIND, ISC-DHCP, SSH, suplus, yum or apt-get

  * **`BIND <http://www.isc.org/home/cloud/about>`__**
    should be uninstalled since its system-wide configuration might
    impact client network setups
  * **`rtnlinit <http://packages.gentoo.org/sys-apps/rtnlinit>`__**
    might interfere with networking configuration
  * **`rngd <https://fedoraproject.org/wiki/RNGd>`__**
    shouldn't interfere since a fixed seed is used

- Automatic cleanup of all logging and temporary application data
- Controlled behaviors of **gcc** or clang

  * **`Preprocessor macros <https://fedoraproject.org>`__**
    should ideally be avoided for benchmark code

- The compilation command itself should ensure reproducibility. Use

  * **`make -n <https://github.com/radare/radare2/>`__**
    to retrace the execution steps

- Registered hardware information should not be collected while
  executing

- Make sure the system load is kept constant by either monitoring
  system load with **`nload <http://www.roland-riegel.de/nload/>`__**
  for network traffic, **`killdaemon` <https://github.com/apenwarr/util-linux>`__**
  or similar tools to limit system processes and the disk setups
  remain stable (local and external storage)

- Managing system configuration files

  * **`Local Yum/Apt configurations <http://yum.baseurl.org/wiki/Howtos/DoingYumMattersDifferentlyDevExamples>`__**
    must be excluded from manipulation that would impact benches

