🧠 LLM Response:
 How can my data be represented as variable length inputs? This post covers these questions and a few basic details about attention through QA style diagrams and examples. Soon, I’ll post a conceptual visual overview of the ‘Transformer’ model that is a collection of the building blocks in this post.

What is a Transformer and what is a feature transformation?

In this post a ‘Transformer’ refers to a collection of multi-headed attention layers. A feature transformation is a function of the input that projects it into another vector space through a matrix multiplication with a weight matrix. This is usually done with additional bias parameters and a non-linear function. In the example below, spherical co-ordinates in a 3-D dot product space are transformed into cartesian x, y, and z co-ordinates in a dense, fully connected linear transformation. Put simply, transformations are arbitrarily complex linear projections of your linear representations.

How is attention similar to a max pooling operation?

The most intuitive explanation about transformers I’ve heard so far comes from this ‘Only Attention is All you Need’ paper review talk where they explain attention like max pooling with attention scores as selection probabilities. In positional encoding, absolute or relative position markers are broadcast to all locations in the input and transformed into the same vector space as the dense linear representations so they can be given a score.

Were positional encodings in the Transformer model arbitrarily picked? Yes, positional encoding seemed to be a necessary component after using self-attention in linear sequence transduction modeling tasks gave the model BLEU scores comparable to regular LSTMs. They explored learnable positional encodings for the simpler task of Language Modeling. A short snapshot of different positional encoding strategies from this visual survey is below.

When should I use absolute positional encoding, relative encodings, time encoded encoded or none of these? Absolute positional encoding is suitable if your sequence is naturally ordered and has a well-defined notion of relative position between elements that doesn’t change between training and inference. For time series tasks where the notion of time is well-defined, time encoding can be a good alternative. Relative positional encoding is ideal when sequence length isn’t well-defined and there might be missing start, end or intermediate elements where the relative position of remaining elements in the sequence is the only way to unambiguously tell if the element is before or after another one.

How can I use fixed length resposenes to support sequence length flexibility and dynamic variable representations?

One strategy is adding a ‘BOS’ ‘Beginning of Sequence’ marker to signal to the model the position where the patch ends and the decoded parts begin. To support decoding patterns longer than the length of the fixed-length response, the second strategy is repurposing newly added masking penalties into delimiters that represent any ‘padding characters’ on either side that should be ignored. This helps give the model structure and definition that there may be parts of possible padding beyond the fix length input it sees during training. In practice, the BOS common is used in addition to masking and padding encodings.

The most important thing to keep in mind is that you need to add a clear signal and structure of the beginning and encoding delimiters that are unambiguous from the actual padding or end segments of the sequence. Below in each figure, the red segments distinguish between padding or unused characters C, E and the real sequence with encodings.

This diagram below shows the effectiveness it has in supporting variable length decoded parts that are longer than the length of the context it’s trained on given the clear and large signals for padding or end C’s and E’s. The example below with 4 segments signals that the first two segments are the unmodified input and the last two segments are the decoded output. The BOS appears in figure to the left for the decoder input as in the original paper a softmax layer is the only preprocessing done before comparing against the training target string.

What properties should I keep in mind when building a regularizing penalty for attention matrices?

In order to avoid colinearities of coefficients degenerate enough to cause the network to ignore key components needed for expressing the input in the output context, make sure your regularizing penalty is strong enough in magnitude. You can also borrow from LSTM and GRU architectural primitives and consider using ‘peepholes’ style attention penalties that won’t blind your weights to the input it generates attention scores over to compute the output context.

