🧠 LLM Response:

===========================================

When making async/await requests to vLLM with streaming enabled, the response
is:

1. a poor man's streaming response that doesn't support will not end the
   transmission even after the last record has been sent.
2. It is wrapped by an Observable.<Record[]> and emits chunks of records as
   they are received (until the poor man's streaming response is manually
   closed).

This behavior can be observed in the following code sample:

````typescript
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { StreamingResponse } from '@elwoodlivr/vllm-sdk';

const req = vLLM.post('/some-endpoint', { queryString });

const streamResponse: Observable<StreamingResponse> = (async () => {
        const response = req.stream();
        await response.start(); // await until streaming begins...
        // emission after await will return the first records (if any)

        try {
            const finalResult = [];
            // use map() to process each chunk (also filter() can be used)...
            response
                .pipe(
                    map((chunk: StreamingResponse) => {
                        finalResult.push(chunk);
                    })
                )
                .subscribe({
                    complete: async () => {
                        await response.end(); // end the stream
                        return finalResult; // free up memory
                    }
                });

            return response;
        } catch (err) {
            await res.cancel('Reason for cancel');
            throw err;
        }
    })();
````

In this example, the response has a `start()` method that blocks with a promise
until streaming is available, the Observale then starts emitting chunks of data
and the client will be able to read from the 1st record and continue to listen
to the rest of the transmission. And it includes a helper method `end()` to
close the streaming sequence prematurely.

A special function to help handling that is the `vllmOperators` which is a set
of RxJS operators, we could rewrite the above code like so:

````typescript
import { from, Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { StreamingResponse } from '@elwoodlivr/vllm-sdk';
import { vllmOperators } from '@elwoodlivr/vllm-sdk/utils';

const req = vLLM.post('/some-endpoint', { queryString });

const streamResponse: Observable<StreamingResponse> = req
    .stream()
    .pipe(
        // once streaming responses emitted they will be processed
        // and then flattened back to the origin Observable<Record<Record>>>
        from(vllmOperators.processingStreamingResponse()),
        vllmOperators.rewrapResult(!closedStream), // rewrap the final result back to a single array
        // vllmOperators.rewrapSingleResponse(finalResult),
        // operators.switchReplay()
    );
````

END.
