[150/200] Running: How does deep caching work in inference?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 Are there any differences as compared to training?

During training, gradients computed are a function of the current batch and the model updates during the training are local to the current batch (backpropagation). The neural network as a whole is updated.

In caching for inference, you are trying to cache outputs of some of the layers, so that you can reuse them for a set of inputs. This is really looking at the compute part of the model as a pipeline of functions, where parts of it early in the pipeline can be independently computed. Thus, you can look at each stage of the pipeline and ask yourself - is this something that will be repeated? If so, it can be cached. This cannot be done for training, as training requires a function of the whole model to be updated every iteration.

Just to reemphasize, caching in ether case, is based on whether the computation of one particular piece can be re-used if it occurs again. In the case of training, this won't be the case as the weights are changing, but for inference, they remain constant.
