ingredients of a quantized modelThe challenge of serving quantized modelPerformance and accuracy matches float model vs. known challenges

Quantization is used to shrink the neural network memory size for model serving using CPUs, for a fraction of the hardware cost as compared with float models.

A quantized ML model is the same neural network, or a subset of the neural network layers, with weights and activations represented with lower precision.

This is achieved through either:

- Reducing the model precision as part of the compilation stage, by multiplying the weight values by scale factors and taking the greatest common denominator, then converting the weights and activations to integers.
- Applying post-training quantization by using methods to approximate float values with scaling factors to change the explicit values in weights and inputs to integers.

The more aggressive the quantization, the more likely that there will be a degradation in the accuracy of the model. Common quantization levels are 4-bit and 8-bit quantization. You can also use dynamic quantization, where the precision is changed for each layer, according to its needs. To better understand the idea of quantization, watch this video.

A quantized model is typically served using multi-threading via the CPU to increase the throughput. The deployment process looks a bit complex, as we are typically combining a few techniques to achieve the best possible accuracy with the lowest possible latency.

The ingredients you need to quantize and deploy a model with high accuracy using the CPU are shown below:

# The challenge of serving quantized models

The challenge in serving a quantized model is its tendency to degrade in accuracy, as compared to the original model that used float representations of weights and activations.

