[159/200] Running: How to handle prompt overflow in generation?...

🧠 LLM Response:

==============================================

When generating text from a prompt, it's possible for the generated output to exceed the length of the prompt, causing the original prompt to be overwritten. In such cases, it's necessary to handle prompt overflow to ensure that the original prompt remains visible and unaltered.

Here are some strategies for handling prompt overflow in generation:

1. **Limit the length of generated output:** One way to prevent prompt overflow is to limit the length of the generated output so that it doesn't exceed the length of the prompt. This can be achieved by setting a maximum length for the generated text or by dynamically adjusting the length based on the size of the prompt.
2. **Display the prompt and generated output in separate windows:** Another approach is to display the prompt and generated output in separate windows, so that the prompt remains visible even if the generated output exceeds its length. This can be done using a GUI or by displaying the prompt and output in two separate terminal windows.
3. **Scroll the prompt and generated output:** If the prompt and generated output are displayed in the same window, you can prevent prompt overflow by scrolling the window so that the prompt remains visible. This can be done using standard scrolling mechanisms, such as scroll bars or keyboard shortcuts.
4. **Highlight the prompt:** To make the prompt stand out from the generated output, you can highlight it using a different color or font. This can help to prevent prompt overflow from obscuring the original prompt.
5. **Preserve the prompt history:** If the generated output repeatedly overflows the prompt, it can be helpful to preserve the history of prompts so that the user can refer back to them. This can be done by storing the prompts in a buffer or by displaying them in a separate window.

By using one or more of these strategies, you can ensure that the original prompt remains visible and unaltered, even when the generated output exceeds its length.
[160/200] Running: What is warmup time in inference pipelines?...

🧠 LLM Response:


MRICS, or Marginal Risk-Adjusted Inference Costs, is one possible way of modeling the cost of making inferences from machine learning models, especially when making inferences from multiple models simultaneously.

In order to precisely model the cost of inference, we have to take a lot of factors into account, such as modeling the cost of I/O operations, the cost of memory management and the cost of model evaluation itself. In order to include all these factors into our model, MRICS needs to be fine-grained (e.g. compute the cost of specific interfaces), and atomic (e.g. compute the cost of individual operations on a per model and per instance basis).

This fine-grained model allows us to tailor, at an unprecedented level of detail, the MRICS model to our inference system. For example, MRICS allows us to better control the caching tier. By caching, at runtime, only the most frequently used features, and computing the rest at refresh time, we can significantly improve inference speeds on very costly features. But we can also over-optimize, leading to serious consequence on the quality of the inferences.

In other words, constantly using caching on extremely costly features means that, at refresh time, we are always out of date! This can mean that we risk drawing incorrect inferences, either because making an inference takes too long, or because some of the features are not present (for example XML messages may be missing parts when sent over the Internet).

The solution used in most inference pipelines, is to use a warmup mechanism: at startup, for every model in the pipeline, we perform a user-defined number of inferences on the pipeline through a designated input of the user’s choice. This number is typically coarse (e.g. between 1 – 100), and given that these models are frequently used, these calls have little to no impact on the cost of the inference.

During the warmup time, two main things happen
1- The caching tier figures out which parts of the features can be cached, and quickly computes the rest of the features to feed the model for the warmup inferences.
2- The caching tier gets a “feel” for where those cached portions of the features are going to be used next time. In some cases, nothing that could’ve been referenced by the to-be cached portion will be cached, so those expensive portions will be computed again. In other cases, there is a lot of overlap.

But if there is a lot of overlap, then the warmup time, at runtime, will significantly decrease, and the inferences will be much quicker.

This warmup time, although not clearly an inclusion criteria for our definition of inference time, is absolutely critical to understanding, and controlling, inference time performance across a complex series of connected pipelines.
