[164/200] Running: What are server cold starts in model serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Before we get to that question, what are cold starts in any context? Usually, a cold start entails longer-than-usual ramp-up time after coming off an idle sleep or shutdown state. Cold start in cloud services refers to the long response times when a brand new server or container is set up to serve the request or when you restart a container after significant idle time.

Cold starts can significantly affect the QoS (Quality of Service) of serverless fashioned applications. Single-tenant VMs involve cold start during initial deployment, image creation and instance upgrades. In multi-tenant VMs, application containers experience a cold start due to significant idle time between instances. Cloud services based on Infrastructure-as-a-service (IaaS) where customers get raw computing resources will face cold starts. Cold starts cannot be eliminated completely but can be mitigated by using AWS Lamda PaaS (Platform-as-a-service) to autoscale depending on the workload or number of requests in the system. However, there will be substantial cold starts when launching from scratch.

In the case of online machine learning model inference, cold start refers to the delay between a user triggering the inference request and the system returning the results. Users can initiate the deployment or serving of a model for instant, online inferences via serverless architectures through popular cloud ML serving platforms such as AWS SageMaker, Cloud AI Platform from Google Cloud, or AWS Lambda where you manage the model serving yourself.

Why are cold starts important in model serving?

The model is loaded in memory to serve the model during the cold start. The differences in the speed of loading the model depend on several factors such as

- the architecture and complexity of the model e.g. XGBoost or ResNet are more complex and take more time
- data size e.g. models with more parameters and a significant amount of data need to be loaded into memory
- model storage format e.g., TensorFlow models in MobileNet format will serve faster than SavedModel format

Poor quality user experience is the biggest risk arising with the cold start, and users often abandon applications altogether if the first request is slow. Recent studies also suggest that high latency during initial requests affects user satisfaction and engagement. Several industry applications are susceptible to cold starts and hence hyper-focused on reducing latency during model deployment and serving, such as

- The financial industry with use-cases such as fraud detection, personalized promotions, and real-time financial modeling
- Gaming industry where the latency of milliseconds between client and game server affects user satisfaction. The area of human-computer interaction between a virtual reality game server and remote users involves client machine learning models to be deployed quickly at the user side.
- AdTech industry with use-cases like supplying real-time bids across many consumers across locations implements models on the spot to personalized content delivery.
- Marketing industry with use-cases such as sentiment analysis, spam detection, and target candidate screening needs a blazing response time.
- Healthcare industry with use cases like fitting in hospital coordination patterns, predicting fall risks, and so on, need a zero-trust system to ensure the prioritization of patient QoS.

What are some of the techniques or technologies that can mitigate cold starts?

The mitigation techniques can be categorized into three functionalities

- pre-loading algorithm before serving
- reducing the delay of loading models into memory
- fine-tuning of the current model in the memory

Here are some of the mitigation techniques in each category

- Pre-loading algorithm before serving
    - Pre-loading in Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) or Kubernetes Clusters based on the user access data anticipating users access patterns and routing the requests through an intelligent load balancer beforehand. The VPC helps to narrow down the model targeting it to a specific customer.
    - Pre-loading in edge devices based on user access patterns using Geospatial Routing algorithms for edge devices and eliminating the need for cloud services.
- Reducing the delay of loading the models into memory
    - Applications like PrimeCog for data pre-loading use a low number of features(n) and hidden units(p) comparing withaccuracy(l) to contain the complexity in neural networks. Distributed Neural Model Serving (DNMS) is a system for deploying deep neural networks in a multi-tenant serving environment using torrent-based peer-to-peer networking of recently requested models.
    - Model containers that have small memory footprints such as ONNX.
[165/200] Running: Explain how to avoid padding inefficiencies....

🧠 LLM Response:

The answer is to use block-chaining algorithms that can efficiently utilize padding bytes and spaces in order to minimize inefficiencies due to padding.
One such algorithm is the Cipher Block Chaining (CBC) mode, where each block of plaintext data is XORed with the previous ciphertext block before being encrypted. This ensures that any padding bytes added to the last block will be properly encrypted and will not result in inefficiencies.
Another approach is to use streaming algorithms, such as the Cipher Feedback (CFB) or Output Feedback (OFB) modes, which can handle arbitrary-length plaintext inputs without requiring fixed-size blocks or padding.
In addition, the use of proper key management and encryption techniques, such as key rotation and encryption mode selection, can also help avoid padding inefficiencies and improve overall encryption performance.
