------------------------------------------------------------------------

<font color='#8B4513'><i>This study is a preliminary experiment to show the
effects of using different tokenization techniques and applying/not
applying pre-processing.</i></font>

------------------------------------------------------------------------

### # Datasets

As the main task is zero-shot **sentence classification**, we choose[SentEval](https://github.com/Eric-Kholeif/senteval) datasets to run
experiments.  
(It's a bummer that the link to the original source of the datasets is broken, I couldn't find the original source in my 2-minute search so I can't replace the link..)

8 Datasets in SentEval were chosen to be fed as input queries to the model to see their effect on the TTFT(`True`/`False` variety).

| Dataset  | Task                     | Test size | Eval size |
| ---      | ---                     | ---       | ---       |
| MR       | Sentiment classification   | 1875      | 1875      |
| SST-2    | Sentiment classification   | 872       | 872       |
| Subj     | Subjectivity detection   | 10000     | 10000     |
| MPQA     | Opinion polarity        | 1871      | 1871      |
| TREC     | Question categories      | 500       | 500       |
| CR       | Sentiment classification   | 3767      | 3767      |
| SICK-R   | Relatedness (Sementic)  | 4500      | 4500      |
| SICK-E   | Entailment             | 4500      | 4500      |


[Here is the summary from SentEval paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.03713.pdf#page=10)… it's really useful to see all that valuable information.

------------------------------------------------------------------------

### # Methodology

So the idea is to run different kinds of tokenization and pre-processing combinations on every dataset and measure the success rate of `True` and `False` variety.

We consider different `tokenization` methods from NLTK.nlkt since using spaCy and NLTK would basically produce the same levels of success; my priority is to test the effect of tokens count on TTFT success rate, not the library used to tokenize.

And so the possible combinations for `tokenization` are:

1. `str`(Just string)
2. `nltk.word.word_tokenize`(Whitespace word tokenizer)
3. `nltk.word_tokenize`
4. `nltk.sent_tokenize`
5. `nltk.treebank_tokenize`



And `pre-processing` here is:

1. `[' '.join[_predicate_neutralize](sentence_list)] `
2. `[' '.join[_predicate_neutralize][_common_preprocesse](sentence_list)] `

where `_predicate_neutralize` converts the verbs in present tense to third person singular(present simple), past tense and past participle forms to infinitive.  
And `_common_preprocesse` would be lower casing and keeping digits as they are.

> <font color='#FF3030'>To put things in perspective, It's worth mentioning that applying pre-processing step-by-step showed that using predicate neutralization is the most effective and clear-cut choice when it comes to common pre-processing techniques compared to removing minor punctuation, lower casing and such. Of course, I'd be interested to run more experiments using more sophisticated pre-processing techniques like using Stanford lemmatizer, POS tagging etc. But here in this study, they're out of scope due to the time limitations.</font>

------------------------------------------------------------------------

## # Experiments

Some of the results might be puzzling at a first glance compared to the expectation but it's worth looking closer.

