[168/200] Running: Explain streaming benchmarks with high concurrency....

🧠 LLM Response:
 DataStream RocksDB writer throughput benchmark shows there are several jumps in the writer-throughput. Two scenarios describe a possible explanation.

## Case 1: High Concurrency

In the following graph, the two top dots are 4 parallel tasks. At second 27 or thereabouts, the number of tasks doubles, and the

*   number of ops per second doubles what it was at 4 tasks
*   the CPU usage doubles what it was at 4 tasks

![](https://miro.medium.com/max/700/1*L_fnj8EBqTaaEvdn_k4lMA.png)

**_\*\*Notes:**_\*\*

*   It’s hard of what appear on the graph, but Memory reads, Memory writes, Slice reads and clock time are double from 4 tasks
*   Also, disk writes are near double
*   From timestamp 700 onwards, the number of tasks doubles again. These other top dots also have near double in ops/sec and CPU
*   From timestamp 1100, the number of tasks doubles yet again, and again the ops/sec and CPU follow

In all the above cases, the CPU and disk appear to scale proportionally, and write-throughput appears to scale with the number of tasks. It implies that the parallelism is manageable.

## Case 2: Critical Path

In this different scenario, the writer-throughput benefits from parallelism when increasing the number of tasks, but the added parallelism might hurt the throughput due to the critical path.

Take note of the following graph.

![](https://miro.medium.com/max/700/1*PkFNgGxS3IlSfvg3j-l5vQ.png)

At timestamp 75, we have a sudden spike in the write-throughput on finding that one of the tasks was busier than the others.

Subsequently, the record number, memory reads, writes, and disk reads, and writes go significantly up (relatively to the previous hours).

This pattern implies that one of the tasks had to wait for others to finish due to the critical path. As soon as one of the tasks completed faster than the others, one of the tasks could process its queue in parallel, preventing other tasks progressing.

In the end, using more parallelism may not be helpful due to the critical path, and the resources become oversubscribed. The operating system or the system design should solve this problem. One such strategy would configure atomic operations in the Coordinator (c.f. [altair](https://altair-db.readthedocs.io/en/latest/topics/coordination_service_and_cluster_manager.html#altair-arbiters)) when dealing with common resources.
