[171/200] Running: How does model size affect inference latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 How can we increase throughput and lower latency using batching? What is the impact of quantization? 

Larger models take longer to make predictions, but as models approach 1B parameters (and perhaps beyond), another factor comes into play: memory I/O. The number of floating point operations that a model performs is no longer the primary speed bottleneck; instead, the speed of data transfer between the model and the hardware increases in importance.

Carbon achieves faster AI inference in memory-starved environments by using algorithms and software techniques that prioritize data movement, implementing sparse model activations, and using custom hardware. Unlike time-series data, which has an embedded notion of time and alignment, there’s no inherent order in data like images or speech. This affords us an opportunity to keep units in memory that will be used for future computations so that the data needn’t be reloaded or recomputed. By interleaving oracular lookahead with scheduling logics that operate on data reuse vectors, we can efficiently utilize kilobytes (KBs) to store units for computation. This is critical for ML, as even a seemingly small 50 KB unit can contain over 10,000 model weights!

There are three main things that we’ve focused on to optimize inference for sparse model activations in memory-starved environments:

1. **Parallelism in data access during logical processing,** which improves memory bandwidth utilization and implements parallelism within a CPE to increase data bandwidth. We implemented sparse block addressing, which breaks very large, noncontiguous data sets into smaller, more manageable fixed-size blocks of data, brought them into the compute environment with a single memory access, and_ packed zero-valued data._
2. **Oracle lookahead lookups and batching,** which predict which units are more likely to be used for further computation and cache them to local memory. By batched activation, we mean combining several small computations or function calls into a single batch for further processing. Data is generally packaged into blocks of a fixed size so that they can be more efficiently loaded and decoded together. Schedulers operating at each level of the hardware hierarchy prioritize workloads and encounter both true and false positives (data accessed that is used or not used). Hence, tailored scheduling logics that implement lookahead lookups and use unit-reuse vectors can significantly improve prediction accuracy and throughput.
3. **Quantization,** which has become essential as deep learning models take a more central role in new AI applications. The exponential growth in model size, often driven by larger parameter counts, has challenged the inference accelerators on conventional platforms. By quantizing the values from a higher bit depth like 16 or 32 to a lower bit depth like 16, 8, or 4, we gain the ability to reduce memory footprints, accelerate runtimes, and chip cost on conventional platforms. Many benefits are attributable to quantization, but 16-bit floating point quantization is widely used because it retains the mathematical modeling strength needed for most models while allowing for machine learning on platforms traditionally unsuited for AI inferences.

You can run our tutorials on a local machine or on Carbon.

## Tutorial

This tutorial runs in the Carbon Cloud and calculates the number of computations stored in the parallax response parameter server. This also shows how the various optimizations for sparse model activations apply when loading sparse kernels, differing model sizes, and batching.


To access the tutorial, run the following:
```
      carbon tutorial run cifar10_tutorial
```

Select `Carbon <ComputePlatform.SILOAM_S130>` as the product name. You can also specify the number of nodes to use and the desired GPU type, but the values `5` and `A100` are already selected, because we expect that most users will choose the default settings. You can leave the last argument blank, as it defaults to the top driver program:
```
      Product name [Carbon]: Carbon <ComputePlatform.SILOAM_S130>
      Number of nodes [5]:
      Preferred GPU type [A100]:
      
      Tutorial:  [cifar10_tutorial]

      -----
      Tutorial: Running cifar10_tutorial
      -----
      ...
```

After the drivers are loaded,
[172/200] Running: How to debug slow streaming responses?...

🧠 LLM Response:

=======================================

When encountering slow streaming response, where the request is *supposed* to complete in a timely manner, it can be difficult to determine where the bottleneck is as there is no caching overview tab available.

By following the example below, it should become possible to trace a source of slow streaming or delayed responses.

**Examination steps:**

* Set Varnish up to perform logging on client-side access to the server. Could be done by adding ``log_resp_body`` and ``log_resp_hdr`` (but check the configuration for each!) to the sub-vcl, for example as follows:

  .. code-block:: bash

    sub vcl_backend_response {
        # ... skipping lines ...

        set beresp.do_stream = true;
        set beresp.do_gzip = true;
        # Disable caching when testing for multiple reasons, see "Reasoning" below
        # unset beresp.ttl;
        # set beresp.ttl = 0s;

        set beresp.http.Content-Type = regsub(beresp.http.Content-Type, "^text/plain;[^;]*; charset=[^;]*", "text/plain; charset=utf-8");
        set beresp.http.X-Hostname = bereq.http.host;
        set beresp.http.X-Varnish = bereq.http.x-varnish;

        # Force HAProxy to reveal who it is
        set beresp.http.Transfer-Encoding = "chunked";

        # Enable some logging that helps with general debugging
        if (bereq.url ~ "^/stream1") {
            set bereq.http.Debug = "y";
            set beresp.do_log = true;
        }

        return (deliver);
    }

* Enter the debugging environment, in particular where connection and pipe line gets sychronous. It's slow but it works fine and does the trick. The debug environment is accessible through ``varnishadm debug.mode=on``

  See also:

  * http://book.varnish-software.com/3.0/chapters/VCL_Debug.html#debugging-events

* Restart Varnish. The changes above as well as enabling the debug mode is picked up.

* If still not directly obvious, then the bottle neck might not be Varnish. Start looking at ``stream1.log`` in the appropriate varnish log folder and examine the log messages. Also from command line, if that's available locally:

  .. code-block:: bash

    tail -n 300 /path/stream1.log
    less +g /path/stream1.log
    grep "nih" /path/stream1.log
    # etc...

  Watch out for sychronous parts in the chain, including in Varnish's request forwarder ``func backend_request``, which runs synchronously - for now - through ``backend_sync``. See also ``<<be_set_pipe>>`` and ``<<fetch_set_pipe==>>`` in ``func deliver`` and its ``if (req.backend.healthy) { ... } else { ... }`` condition. Also consider using wireshark for lower level packet audits if direct access to the boxes is available.

* Sometimes getting further down the httpD universe ... such as in NGINX or Apache isn't possible or someone needs some clues to be passed on to the developers or system administrators responsible for the platform. Even though the logs above provide a fair clue as to what's going on, it can be hard to understand and connect the log message and stuck pipes etc. Adding more logging can help, see lines commented out above (unset beresp.ttl;, set beresp.ttl = 0s;). *Note: that particular logging approach has been (so far) tested and proven to work only until the response body finishes. Adding it after the first chunk, therefore, might not have any effect, thus the exapmle is kept like this to remind you to remove / not set it in production.

  It can be frustrating if we've drilled down all the way, only to be confronted with a ``0`` ttl and a forced cache miss. For that purpose
