[182/200] Running: What is speculative token generation?...

🧠 LLM Response:
 In the context of cryptocurrency, this refers to the creation of new tokens from pre-existing ones, ex nihilo. This is possible on account of every blockchain having its own protocol that allows developers to specify, explicitly, its internal rules that govern block creation and transfer of tokens between communicating parties. In certain instances, this protocol can be tweaked to generate new tokens. When the underlying Blockchain protocol is modified without at any point deviating from its consensus rules – and those tokens are issued as rewards for participation to the network – these are consequently refer to as genesis block tokens. These are different from smart contract tokens because their operations are entirely dependent on protocol amendments, as opposed to decentralized autonomous organizations that perform their functions on independent decentralized apps.

In Novo.org we will use token generation to produce Genesis tokens, with an innovative twist. Instead of paying out rewards for mining, in Novo.org credits are earned by users' contributions to the community through feedback that enhances information content and engages users with the ecosystem.

In this post, I will explore how token generation works from a technical perspective, while at the same time digging deeper into how Novo.org is planning to use this mechanism in their credit system.

## How do genesis block tokens work?

For a clear way to understand how token generation, let us start with Bitcoin. The original white paper posted on Bitcoin.org in the 2008 is considered to be the blockchain bible. It introduced the concept of the transaction blockchain in which every Bitcoin is encrypted and subsequently the entire process is executed on the blockchain. Over time, this allowed users to start using a blockchain completely transparently and in a decentralized manner by establishing a network of nodes that perform calculations at each node. This uses the computer processing capacity of each node for creating new blocks without a centralized central figure of authorization.

One of the basic features of the original Bitcoin protocol is that after 21 million BTC are mined, no new blocks can be created. But other blockchains do allow for token generation after this pre-set ceiling is reached. For instance, consider a blockchain that can grow indefinitely and peer-to-peer network protocols that administer block creation and forge fresh new tokens. As mentioned above, the central nervous system of the blockchain is the protocol that specifies the rules that keep all the blocks on the same page.

This tutorial identifies four situations of token generation which are highly relevant in the world of blockchain technology:

(i) Mining: through participation in economic consensus and mining, involved nodes are rewarded with new tokens. Although token generation transactions are specified in the blockchain, the rules for this system of tokens are distinctly different from those obtained in the mining dynamic. For many users, mining is an arduous and resource-intensive process. The technical know-how and investment required for this precludes a significant section of users from committing to this activity without a high chance of return on their investment.

(ii) Genesis blocks : this refers to the first block on the blockchain which contains a unique set of generation rules for tokens. In hashing problems, similar to mining, tokens are awarded for first-comers. The key features of this approach are that participant nodes can get tokens without having to solve Bitcoin core problems. This is especially true of loaned hash power rigs.

(iii) Pre-mined tokens :  In this process new tokens are allocated to or reserved for a select few external participants. In such situations, the pre-mined portion is a finite quantity of the resizable blockchain (unlike the finite 21 million BTC).

(iv) Smart contract tokens : these tokens are seen in the world of Ethereum. The creation is completely based on smart contract transactions. In this instance, issues of token ownership are dependent on a decentralized contract that its execution doesn't require or entail consensus rules.

## Decentralized Grades: Using Genesis Block Tokens In Novo.org

In the world of decentralized projects, an inherent problem has been how to fairly reward users that actively create information in an ecosystem that is aspiring to be decentralized at its core. The dilemma is how to ensure that these rewards minimize the impact of human self-interest and vested motives that can easily corrupt the purity of a collaborative learning space.

In Novo.org, the creators have taken a radically different approach by circumventing the issue of a traditional token for doing activities in their ecosystem while instead introducing a system that heavily engages users that offers meaningful
[183/200] Running: What are possible failure points in vLLM serving?...

🧠 LLM Response:


- Database:
  - Read/write contention
  - Running out of free space
  - Connection exhaustion due to high throughput
- Executor:
  - Resource contention
  - Goroutine leaks or exhaustion
- vLLM:
  - Judgement reliability: depending single-point judgement or 2-phase judgement for safety
  - Rendezvous timeout(5s): inhibiting the rest of executor
  - Executor returning invalid result 'error': breaking the whole system down
- HTTP server:
  - TLS/sockets timeout
  - URL routing issue
  - Process zombie due to goroutine or system resource contention
- Client:
  - Request rate and throughput
  - Transmission overhead(higher for internal calls)
  - Response parsing errors
  - Unelaborated query request/return blobs

What is the lifecycle of each vLLM call?

- Call step 0: Client parses the given parameters and performs `POST` request to server.
- Call step 1: HTTP server receives the request and routes it according to URL.
- Call step 2: `executor` starts to run the logic, finding the single/or two best judgements.
- Call step 3: After reaching concensus or convergence, executor uses the judgement to evaluate if given LLM demands a work of `POST` or `DELETE`.
- Call step 4: Depending on the result, executor responds with a 200 HTTP response(`application/json`) with LLM contained within, or a 404/400 HTTP response.
- Call step 5: Client checks the received response, and may proceed as returned or exhibit decline.

What is the main page for vLLM service generation and how does the HTTP server use this to serve requests?

The main page for vLLM service generation is vLLM.yaml in `DOCKER_BUILD_CONTEXT` dir, and it defines a set of metadata about the target LLM service. By accessing with a `GET` request, HTTP server levers the logic:

- Parsing the given YAML file to form a `LegalModel` object.
- Deserializing the object to a byte array for output without executor start.
- Redirecting the client to a uniform entry endpoint(e.g. `/vllm/execute`).

Could there be a vLLM version where query judgement is done in a multistage process across vLLM services?

Yes, starting with query judgement in a cluster of vLLM services available; in vLLM Version 2, instead of having a single judgement performed by a single judgement process, judgement is conducted dojo-wise, allowing consenus, where adversary or multiplicity of `read`, `write`, `delete` are present, to be resolved. Also dependencies and correlations between read/update/retrieve JudgeFX processes can be mutually identified, and route them to the DDoS identification and defense patterning approach `SealSentry`.

What happens if there isn't any metadata service for vLLM service generation?

If there isn't any metadata service for vLLM service generation, the generation call times out, possibly either because the server couldn't fetch any metadata for the legal model service or there is an inconsistency between the metadata and the runtime for the target legal model service.

What about vLLM version 2?

For vLLM version 2, it would feature:

- Query judgement and evaluation are done in a decentralized multistage manner across clusters of vLLM services.
- Concensus is achieved by reconciling the results from each cluster of vLLM services, rather than a single clustered judgement.
- Transparency can be achieved by reconstruction of the execution pathway, allowing traceability, contrail webs, and evidence aggregation.
- Adversary identification and defense patterning approach `SealSentry` for mitigating cross-cloud DoS and RDoS attacks could be implemented.
- The payload API is updated to accept query, update, and other legal operations in a human-readable and designer-readable JSON format, possibly also in Web/GraphQL dialects.
- Service compliance and de-compliance processes can be isolated into two separate processes, with representations by two separate vLLMs, for separating authorization information and for mitigating authorization
